{"bendscript":"0.1","blocks":[{"id":"blk-75508","kind":"heading","level":1,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106843","text":"Skill 07 — Cognitive Science Foundations"}]},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75510","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106844","text":"Research grounding for each of the ten protocols: the theories, the papers, the design constraints, and the open questions. Eight cognitive primitives grounded in cognitive science / neuroscience, plus two cross-cutting protocols (OS-009 PRISM, OS-010 PULSE) grounded in psychometrics and closed-loop control theory."}]}],"id":"blk-75509","kind":"quote"},{"id":"blk-75511","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106845","text":"Why This Matters"}]},{"id":"blk-75512","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106846","text":"OpenSentience protocols are not arbitrary architecture decisions. Each one maps to a specific cognitive science finding about how intelligent systems manage knowledge, attention, and self-regulation. Understanding the research helps you understand "},{"id":"spn-106847","marks":["italic"],"text":"why"},{"id":"spn-106848","text":" the protocols work the way they do and where the boundaries of the analogy lie."}]},{"id":"blk-75513","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106849","text":"OS-001: Hippocampal Consolidation"}]},{"id":"blk-75514","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106850","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106851","text":" McClelland, McNaughton & O'Reilly (1995). \"Why there are complementary learning systems in the hippocampus and neocortex.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75515","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106852","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106853","text":" The brain uses two complementary systems for memory. The hippocampus rapidly encodes new experiences (fast write). The neocortex slowly integrates this knowledge into long-term structure (consolidation). Replaying memories during sleep transfers hippocampal traces into neocortical representations without catastrophic forgetting."}]},{"id":"blk-75516","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106854","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-001:"}]},{"id":"blk-75517","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75519","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106855","text":"New knowledge must be stored immediately without disrupting existing memory"}]}],"id":"blk-75518","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75521","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106856","text":"Background consolidation must merge, decay, and prune to maintain quality"}]}],"id":"blk-75520","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75523","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106857","text":"Episodic memories (events) are stored differently from semantic memories (facts)"}]}],"id":"blk-75522","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75525","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106858","text":"Confidence decays over time unless reinforced by retrieval or outcomes"}]}],"id":"blk-75524","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75526","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106859","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75527","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75529","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106860","text":"Immediate "},{"id":"spn-106861","marks":["code"],"text":"store_node"},{"id":"spn-106862","text":" writes (hippocampal fast-write analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75528","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75531","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106863","marks":["code"],"text":"run_consolidation"},{"id":"spn-106864","text":" background cycle (neocortical integration analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75530","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75533","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106865","text":"Three node types mapping to memory systems: episodic, semantic, procedural"}]}],"id":"blk-75532","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75534","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106866","text":"OS-002: Working Memory Gating"}]},{"id":"blk-75535","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106867","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106868","text":" O'Reilly & Frank (2006). \"Making working memory work: A computational model of learning in the prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75536","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106869","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106870","text":" The prefrontal cortex maintains active representations in working memory, but not everything gets in. The basal ganglia acts as a gate — deciding what information is relevant enough to enter working memory based on learned relevance signals and conflict detection."}]},{"id":"blk-75537","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106871","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-002:"}]},{"id":"blk-75538","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75540","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106872","text":"Not all retrieved knowledge should be acted upon equally"}]}],"id":"blk-75539","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75542","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106873","text":"Cyclic knowledge (contradictions, self-reinforcing claims) needs detection"}]}],"id":"blk-75541","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75544","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106874","text":"The kappa parameter serves as the conflict signal: kappa = 0 means no"}]}],"id":"blk-75543","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75545","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106875","text":"conflict (open the gate), kappa > 0 means conflict (engage deliberation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75546","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75548","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106876","text":"Routing must be fast for the common case (acyclic) and thorough for the"}]}],"id":"blk-75547","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75549","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106877","text":"rare case (cyclic)"}]},{"id":"blk-75550","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106878","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75551","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75553","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106879","text":"SCC detection on retrieved neighborhoods (basal ganglia conflict signal)"}]}],"id":"blk-75552","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75555","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106880","text":"Kappa computation (gating threshold)"}]}],"id":"blk-75554","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75557","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106881","text":"Fast vs deliberate routing based on kappa value"}]}],"id":"blk-75556","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75558","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106882","text":"OS-003: Dual-Process Theory"}]},{"id":"blk-75559","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106883","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106884","text":" Kahneman (2011). \"Thinking, Fast and Slow.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75560","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106885","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106886","text":" Human cognition operates in two modes. System 1 is fast, automatic, and heuristic-based — it handles routine decisions effortlessly. System 2 is slow, effortful, and analytical — it engages when System 1 encounters novelty, conflict, or high stakes."}]},{"id":"blk-75561","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106887","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-003:"}]},{"id":"blk-75562","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75564","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106888","text":"Most agent decisions should be fast (System 1 analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75563","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75566","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106889","text":"Deliberation should engage only when triggered by conflict signals (kappa > 0)"}]}],"id":"blk-75565","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75568","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106890","text":"The deliberation protocol must structure argumentation (bid, debate, vote,"}]}],"id":"blk-75567","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75569","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106891","text":"commit) to resolve conflict rather than amplify it"}]},{"id":"blk-75570","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75572","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106892","text":"Timeout mechanisms prevent deliberation from stalling indefinitely"}]}],"id":"blk-75571","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75573","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106893","marks":["bold"],"text":"What AgenTroMatic implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75574","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75576","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106894","text":"Four-phase deliberation pipeline (bid/debate/vote/commit)"}]}],"id":"blk-75575","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75578","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106895","text":"Argumentation framework with claims, warrants, and rebuttals"}]}],"id":"blk-75577","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75580","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106896","text":"Consensus and escalation mechanisms"}]}],"id":"blk-75579","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75581","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106897","text":"OS-004: Endogenous Attention"}]},{"id":"blk-75582","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106898","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106899","text":" Desimone & Duncan (1995). \"Neural mechanisms of selective visual attention.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75583","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106900","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106901","text":" Attention is not just stimulus-driven (exogenous — a loud noise grabs your attention). It is also goal-directed (endogenous — you look for your car keys because you intend to drive). Endogenous attention biases perception toward goal-relevant information, filtering out distractions."}]},{"id":"blk-75584","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106902","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-004:"}]},{"id":"blk-75585","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75587","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106903","text":"The attention engine must prioritize based on active goals, not just recency"}]}],"id":"blk-75586","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75588","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106904","text":"or novelty"}]},{"id":"blk-75589","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75591","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106905","text":"Salience scoring combines urgency, recency, goal relevance, and confidence"}]}],"id":"blk-75590","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75593","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106906","text":"The survey/triage/dispatch cycle mirrors the scan/filter/act structure of"}]}],"id":"blk-75592","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75594","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106907","text":"biological attention"}]},{"id":"blk-75595","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75597","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106908","text":"Dispatch modes (act, learn, escalate, idle) prevent attention from always"}]}],"id":"blk-75596","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75598","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106909","text":"demanding action"}]},{"id":"blk-75599","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106910","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75600","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75602","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106911","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_survey"},{"id":"spn-106912","text":" (endogenous scan of goal-relevant state)"}]}],"id":"blk-75601","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75604","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106913","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_run_cycle"},{"id":"spn-106914","text":" (full survey/triage/dispatch loop)"}]}],"id":"blk-75603","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75606","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106915","text":"Goal bias in salience scoring"}]}],"id":"blk-75605","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75607","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106916","text":"OS-005: Resource Rationality"}]},{"id":"blk-75608","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106917","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106918","text":" Lieder & Griffiths (2020). \"Resource-rational analysis: Understanding human cognition as the optimal use of limited computational resources.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75609","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106919","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106920","text":" Optimal cognition is not about always computing the best answer. It is about allocating computational effort proportional to the value of the decision. Simple decisions warrant fast, cheap heuristics. High-stakes decisions justify expensive, thorough analysis."}]},{"id":"blk-75610","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106921","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-005:"}]},{"id":"blk-75611","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75613","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106922","text":"Three model tiers map to three levels of computational investment"}]}],"id":"blk-75612","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75615","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106923","text":"The same tool surface and topology are available at every tier"}]}],"id":"blk-75614","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75617","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106924","text":"Escalation rules define when to upgrade from cheap to expensive processing"}]}],"id":"blk-75616","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75619","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106925","text":"Budget constraints (tokens, latency, cost) prevent unbounded computation"}]}],"id":"blk-75618","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75620","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106926","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous and Agentelic implement:"}]},{"id":"blk-75621","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75623","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106927","text":"local_small / local_large / cloud_frontier tier definitions"}]}],"id":"blk-75622","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75625","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106928","text":"Escalation thresholds based on confidence and decision stakes"}]}],"id":"blk-75624","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75627","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106929","text":"Token and latency budgets per tier"}]}],"id":"blk-75626","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75628","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106930","text":"OS-006: Executive Function"}]},{"id":"blk-75629","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106931","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106932","text":" Miyake, Friedman et al. (2000). \"The unity and diversity of executive functions and their contributions to complex frontal lobe tasks.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75630","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106933","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106934","text":" Executive function is an umbrella term for the cognitive processes that regulate, control, and manage other cognitive processes. Miyake et al. identified three core components: inhibitory control (suppressing inappropriate responses), task switching (flexibly shifting between tasks), and working memory updating (monitoring and revising held information)."}]},{"id":"blk-75631","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106935","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-006:"}]},{"id":"blk-75632","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75634","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106936","text":"Inhibitory control maps to the permission system (blocking disallowed actions)"}]}],"id":"blk-75633","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75636","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106937","text":"Task switching maps to the lifecycle state machine (managing agent state)"}]}],"id":"blk-75635","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75638","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106938","text":"Working memory updating maps to graduated autonomy (adapting the level of"}]}],"id":"blk-75637","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75639","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106939","text":"agent independence based on accumulated trust)"}]},{"id":"blk-75640","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75642","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106940","text":"The audit trail provides metacognitive monitoring — awareness of what the"}]}],"id":"blk-75641","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75643","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106941","text":"system has done"}]},{"id":"blk-75644","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106942","marks":["bold"],"text":"What `open_sentience` implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75645","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75647","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106943","text":"PermissionEngine (inhibitory control)"}]}],"id":"blk-75646","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75649","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106944","text":"AgentLifecycle GenStateMachine (task switching)"}]}],"id":"blk-75648","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75651","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106945","text":"AutonomyController (working memory updating / cognitive flexibility)"}]}],"id":"blk-75650","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75653","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106946","text":"AuditWriter (metacognitive monitoring)"}]}],"id":"blk-75652","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75654","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106947","text":"OS-007: Adversarial Robustness — Self/Non-Self Discrimination"}]},{"id":"blk-75655","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106948","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theoretical grounding:"},{"id":"spn-106949","text":" Adaptive immunity theory (Burnet 1959; Janeway 1989)."}]},{"id":"blk-75656","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106950","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106951","text":" Biological immune systems distinguish self from non-self through a combination of innate pattern recognition and adaptive memory. The same architecture maps cleanly to agent threat detection: known-good behavior is \"self,\" novel attack patterns are \"non-self,\" and the system must learn to recognize new threats without misclassifying legitimate variation."}]},{"id":"blk-75657","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106952","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-007:"}]},{"id":"blk-75658","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75660","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106953","text":"Five threat categories with explicit detection rules"}]}],"id":"blk-75659","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75662","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106954","text":"Defense protocols must be reversible (quarantine before destroy)"}]}],"id":"blk-75661","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75664","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106955","text":"Integration with OS-006 for permission revocation and OS-008 for circuit"}]}],"id":"blk-75663","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75665","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106956","text":"breaking — the immune response is enacted by the governance and harness layers"}]},{"id":"blk-75666","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106957","text":"OS-008: Supervisory Attentional System"}]},{"id":"blk-75667","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106958","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-106959","text":" Norman & Shallice (1986). \"Attention to action: Willed and automatic control of behavior.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75668","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106960","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106961","text":" Routine behavior runs automatically through contention scheduling between learned action schemas. A "},{"id":"spn-106962","marks":["italic"],"text":"supervisory attentional system"},{"id":"spn-106963","text":" intervenes when novel, dangerous, or constraint-violating situations arise. The supervisory system does not execute behavior directly — it modulates which schemas are allowed to run."}]},{"id":"blk-75669","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106964","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-008:"}]},{"id":"blk-75670","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75672","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106965","text":"The harness is the runtime that calls the agent, not a tool the agent calls"}]}],"id":"blk-75671","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75674","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106966","text":"It enforces pipeline ordering, quality gates, sprint contracts, and context"}]}],"id":"blk-75673","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75675","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106967","text":"management"}]},{"id":"blk-75676","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75678","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106968","text":"It intervenes when prerequisites are not met or when quality thresholds are"}]}],"id":"blk-75677","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75679","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106969","text":"not crossed — but it does not generate the agent's outputs"}]},{"id":"blk-75680","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106970","text":"OS-009: PRISM — Meta-Cognition + Psychometrics"}]},{"id":"blk-75681","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106971","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-75682","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75684","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106972","text":"Rasch, G. (1960). \"Probabilistic models for some intelligence and attainment"}]}],"id":"blk-75683","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75685","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106973","text":"tests.\" (Item Response Theory foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75686","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75688","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106974","text":"Lord, F. M. (1980). \"Applications of item response theory to practical testing"}]}],"id":"blk-75687","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75689","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106975","text":"problems.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75690","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75692","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106976","text":"Green, D. M. & Swets, J. A. (1966). \"Signal detection theory and"}]}],"id":"blk-75691","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75693","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106977","text":"psychophysics.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75694","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75696","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106978","text":"Flavell, J. H. (1979). \"Metacognition and cognitive monitoring.\" (Meta-cognition)"}]}],"id":"blk-75695","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75697","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106979","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-106980","text":" Measuring whether a system "},{"id":"spn-106981","marks":["italic"],"text":"learns"},{"id":"spn-106982","text":" (rather than merely "},{"id":"spn-106983","marks":["italic"],"text":"answers"},{"id":"spn-106984","text":") requires the same toolkit psychometricians built for measuring human learning: calibrated item difficulty, separation of item-quality from learner-quality parameters, and detection of response bias. PRISM applies IRT to scenario calibration and signal detection theory to dimension scoring, then closes the meta-cognitive loop by reflecting on its own scenarios and evolving them."}]},{"id":"blk-75698","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106985","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-009:"}]},{"id":"blk-75699","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75701","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106986","text":"9 continual-learning dimensions (retention, plasticity, transfer,"}]}],"id":"blk-75700","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75702","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106987","text":"contradiction handling, etc.) — each with calibrated scoring rubrics"}]},{"id":"blk-75703","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75705","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106988","text":"4-phase evaluation loop: compose → interact → observe → reflect → diagnose"}]}],"id":"blk-75704","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75706","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106989","text":"(the diagnostic must itself be a closed loop)"}]},{"id":"blk-75707","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75709","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106990","text":"BYOR ingestion — point PRISM at any repo and it generates scenarios"}]}],"id":"blk-75708","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75711","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106991","text":"IRT calibration of scenario difficulty across cycles"}]}],"id":"blk-75710","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75713","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106992","marks":["bold"],"text":"PULSE-aware:"},{"id":"spn-106993","text":" PRISM's "},{"id":"spn-106994","marks":["code"],"text":"interact"},{"id":"spn-106995","text":" phase reads any system's PULSE manifest"}]}],"id":"blk-75712","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75714","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106996","text":"at runtime and drives the inner loop through its declared phases"}]},{"id":"blk-75715","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-106997","text":"OS-010: PULSE — Closed-Loop Control Theory + Temporal Cognition"}]},{"id":"blk-75716","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106998","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-75717","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75719","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-106999","text":"Wiener, N. (1948). \"Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal"}]}],"id":"blk-75718","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75720","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107000","text":"and the Machine.\" (Closed-loop control foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75721","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75723","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107001","text":"Allen, J. F. (1983). \"Maintaining knowledge about temporal intervals.\""}]}],"id":"blk-75722","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75724","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107002","text":"(Interval algebra)"}]},{"id":"blk-75725","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75727","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107003","text":"CloudEvents v1 specification (CNCF, 2019). (Event envelope standard)"}]}],"id":"blk-75726","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75728","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107004","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107005","text":" Wiener's cybernetics established that intelligent behavior — in animals or machines — requires closed feedback loops with explicit phases: sense, decide, act, observe, adjust. Allen's interval algebra formalized the 13 possible relationships between temporal intervals, providing a vocabulary for describing how loops can nest, overlap, and signal one another. PULSE combines these into a manifest standard: every loop in the [&] portfolio declares its phases in the same vocabulary, and cross-loop signals use CloudEvents v1 envelopes so that loops can compose without bespoke adapters."}]},{"id":"blk-75729","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107006","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-010:"}]},{"id":"blk-75730","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75732","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107007","text":"5 canonical phase kinds ("},{"id":"spn-107008","marks":["code"],"text":"retrieve"},{"id":"spn-107009","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107010","marks":["code"],"text":"route"},{"id":"spn-107011","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107012","marks":["code"],"text":"act"},{"id":"spn-107013","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107014","marks":["c